# Non-secret deploy settings, sourced by env.sh. Checked in so every deploy -- from GitHub Actions
# or a laptop -- sets the same environment: `gcloud run deploy --set-env-vars` replaces a service's
# whole environment, so a value that only lives in one person's shell gets dropped by the next
# deploy from anywhere else. Secrets stay in Secret Manager (README.md "Secret Manager").
#
# Each line keeps an already-exported value, so `OUTFITTER_OPERATORS=... ./release.sh` still
# overrides for one run. Change a value here (and merge) to change it for good.

# The laptop LM Studio tunnel (tunnel.sh in the repo root); the E2.8 preflight probes it. Access to
# it is gated by LOCAL_TUNNEL_TOKEN (a secret), not by this name being private.
TUNNEL_DOMAIN_LMSTUDIO="${TUNNEL_DOMAIN_LMSTUDIO:-mgm-lmstudio.ngrok.app}"
LITELLM_BASE_URL="${LITELLM_BASE_URL:-https://router.kernal.one/v1}"
PROVIDER_POLL_MS="${PROVIDER_POLL_MS:-300000}"
IDLE_STOP_SECONDS="${IDLE_STOP_SECONDS:-1800}"

# Which SandboxProvider new runs launch on (config.ts's SandboxProviderName); service.sh reads
# this instead of hard-coding cloudrun, so a GKE switch (docs/specs/gke-sandbox-provider-plan.md)
# is "edit this line, tag a release" (no gke-switch.sh; deploy/README.md step 6). Same override caveat
# as everywhere else in this file: a laptop release run with SANDBOX_PROVIDER already exported in
# the shell ships that value instead of the default below. A tag-triggered deploy from GitHub
# Actions starts from a clean environment and never exports it, so it always gets this file's value.
# gke since 2026-09-26 (epic #134). Runs already started on Cloud Run keep their recorded provider
# (Run.sandboxProvider) and are still stopped through it: createProviders registers cloudrun next to
# the default whenever CLOUDRUN_JOB is set, which service.sh always does. Rollback: set this back to
# cloudrun and tag a release.
SANDBOX_PROVIDER="${SANDBOX_PROVIDER:-gke}"

# GKE Agent Sandbox provider settings (docs/specs/gke-sandbox-provider-plan.md §2, §3 PR3 row).
# GKE_CLUSTER empty keeps GKE fully off: service.sh only adds the GKE_* env vars below to a deploy
# when it's set, so a deploy with it empty ships the exact same environment as before these lines
# existed. Switching providers is "SANDBOX_PROVIDER=gke here, tag a release" -- see
# deploy/README.md "GKE provider (optional)".
# On since 2026-09-24 (epic #134): gke-iam.sh and ci-setup.sh ran first, so every release now runs
# gke-template.sh with the deployer's access in place. New runs launch on GKE since 2026-09-26
# (SANDBOX_PROVIDER above).
# One dash, unlike the lines around it: an unset GKE_CLUSTER gets the default, but an exported empty
# one stays empty, so `GKE_CLUSTER= ./release.sh` (or a test) can still turn GKE off for one run.
GKE_CLUSTER="${GKE_CLUSTER-outfitter}"
# ${REGION:-us-central1}, not ${REGION}: config.env must stay sourceable under `set -u` on its own
# (no env.sh, which is the only other place REGION is ever set) -- the GitHub Actions workflow does
# exactly that to decide whether to install kubectl, and REGION being unbound there must never abort
# the step before it even gets to deciding that. us-central1 matches env.sh's own REGION default.
GKE_LOCATION="${GKE_LOCATION:-${REGION:-us-central1}}"
GKE_NAMESPACE="${GKE_NAMESPACE:-outfitter-sandboxes}"
GKE_WARM_POOL="${GKE_WARM_POOL:-outfitter-agent}"
# warm since 2026-09-26: PR4's warm assignment (#156) shipped in outfitter-v2026.09.24.4 and was
# verified on a claimed warm pod (about 2 s from claim to agentd's "assigned"). Outside the warm
# hours (gke-schedule.sh) the pool is at 0 and the controller builds a fresh pod with the claim's
# annotations already on it (agent-sandbox v1.0.3 sandboxclaim_controller.go:1730), i.e. a cold start.
GKE_LAUNCH_MODE="${GKE_LAUNCH_MODE:-warm}"
GKE_MAX_ACTIVE="${GKE_MAX_ACTIVE:-3}"
# Sandbox pod memory, requests == limits (sandbox-template.yaml). 4Gi since #187: 2Gi (copied from
# the Cloud Run job) left ~1.8 GiB for the agent's own commands once gVisor took its share, and a
# build or test suite OOM-killed a run mid-turn. On 1 vCPU, Autopilot allows 1-6.5 GiB (gke-template.sh
# refuses anything outside 1Gi-6656Mi, since Autopilot would silently add CPU instead). The extra
# 2 GiB costs about $0.01/h per sandbox (spec's rates). gke-template.sh ships it with the release.
GKE_SANDBOX_MEMORY="${GKE_SANDBOX_MEMORY:-4Gi}"

# Drive previews (#217, docs/specs/drive-previews.md): the registrable domain previews are served on
# (https://<id>.kernal.run, an ngrok wildcard; the zone kernal-run lives in cognitio-flow). Never
# under kernal.one. Empty turns previews off. Both roles; the api also needs NGROK_API_KEY.
PREVIEW_DOMAIN="${PREVIEW_DOMAIN-kernal.run}"

# Web voice (#252, epic #256), web role only: the Deepgram project that outfitter-deepgram-key (a
# Member key, in Secret Manager) belongs to. Kernal's bundle key is in the same project, so web and
# laptop voice share one bill. Not secret; #257 reads per-person usage from it.
DEEPGRAM_PROJECT_ID="${DEEPGRAM_PROJECT_ID-0d8ef213-4d9d-4367-acf8-9968d17cafd7}"

# Atlas Agent Engine (E9, docs/specs/atlas-agent-engine-and-a2a.md §2), link role only (the admin
# socket's ops and, later, A2A run there). Each part is off while its project id is empty; its
# service account's credentials are Secret Manager secrets service.sh mounts when they exist
# (outfitter-agentengine-memory-sa / outfitter-agentengine-agents-sa, JSON {client_id,
# client_secret}). *_SA_EXPIRES_AT is the secret's expiry from the create or rotate response (a
# service account can't read its own); the api warns from 14 days before. Not secret.
# Memory: the Atlas project `outfitter` itself, memory-only (owner decision 2026-10-01). Its service
# account has role AGENT_DEVELOPER, which is enough for every memory route (checked live
# 2026-10-01); PROJECT_OWNER isn't needed. Nothing reads memory before E9 PR3.
AGENT_ENGINE_BASE_URL="${AGENT_ENGINE_BASE_URL:-https://agentengine.mongodb.com}"
AGENT_ENGINE_MEMORY_PROJECT_ID="${AGENT_ENGINE_MEMORY_PROJECT_ID-6aafddf75566cf48726735bb}"
AGENT_ENGINE_MEMORY_SA_EXPIRES_AT="${AGENT_ENGINE_MEMORY_SA_EXPIRES_AT-2026-12-30T01:51:48Z}"
# Agents: a separate Atlas project (outfitter-agents), created for E9 PR4. Empty until then.
AGENT_ENGINE_AGENTS_PROJECT_ID="${AGENT_ENGINE_AGENTS_PROJECT_ID-}"
AGENT_ENGINE_AGENTS_SA_EXPIRES_AT="${AGENT_ENGINE_AGENTS_SA_EXPIRES_AT-}"

# Web role only. ';'-separated: --set-env-vars splits its value on commas.
OUTFITTER_OPERATORS="${OUTFITTER_OPERATORS:-mgm@mgms.eu;matej@mgms.eu}"

# Who IAP admits to outfitter-api, and the custom OAuth client that lets members outside the
# mgmonteleone.com org sign in (README.md "IAP access for users outside the org"; the client secret
# is in Secret Manager). access.sh applies IAP_MEMBERS to IAP (never the routine deploy);
# service.sh also turns it, plus the operators, into PREVIEW_ALLOW and OUTFITTER_LOGIN_ALLOW (who
# may sign a Kernal in, #390). A change here reaches IAP only once the owner runs access.sh.
IAP_MEMBERS="${IAP_MEMBERS:-domain:mgmonteleone.com,domain:serviam-it.hr,user:matija.turk@gmail.com,user:matej@mgms.eu}"
IAP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID="${IAP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID:-97916961297-ul9tkt3hh0l8i16imltqcarfrkat1k17.apps.googleusercontent.com}"

# e2e-identity.sh only: the service account live tests sign in through IAP as (#219), and who may
# mint its ID tokens (','-separated user:/group:/serviceAccount: principals). It has no project
# roles; IAP admits it to outfitter-api and the app treats it as an ordinary, non-sysop user.
E2E_SA_NAME="${E2E_SA_NAME:-outfitter-e2e}"
E2E_TOKEN_MINTERS="${E2E_TOKEN_MINTERS:-user:mgm@mgms.eu}"
# The one GitHub workflow allowed to mint those tokens in CI, only at an outfitter-v* release tag,
# through its own Workload Identity pool (never ci-setup.sh's deploy pool: that pool's principals
# can act as the deployer). Empty skips setting it up; it doesn't undo a setup already applied
# (README.md "Live-test identity" has the revoke steps).
E2E_GITHUB_WORKFLOW="${E2E_GITHUB_WORKFLOW-.github/workflows/outfitter-live.yml}"
